questions.description:
Each fetch takes about 200 ms.

```js
async function loadDashboard(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);  // ~200 ms
  const config = await fetchConfig();    // ~200 ms, does NOT depend on `user`
  return { user, config };
}
```

questions.explanation:
`await` pauses the function until the promise settles. `fetchConfig()` isn't even **called** until `fetchUser` resolves, so the two independent requests run one after the other, about 200 ms + 200 ms = **400 ms**. Running independent requests in sequence like this is a *request waterfall*, one of the most common avoidable causes of slow screens.

To fix it, start both requests before you await, so they run at the same time:

```js
async function loadDashboard(userId) {
  const [user, config] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(userId),
    fetchConfig(),
  ]);
  return { user, config }; // ~200 ms
}
```

The other options don't help. Sequential `await`s don't run in parallel. `useMemo` caches a computed value across renders but doesn't change the timing of two awaits in one call. And `staleTime` only helps when you read data that's already cached, so it can't speed up a cold first load.

